      "id": 1,
      "text": "Kilgore was founded as Boulware in 1883 when the Fremont, Elkhorn and Missouri Valley Railroad reached the spot.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kilgore%2C_Nebraska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1904 the village changed its name from Georgia to Kilgore after the post office objected to the duplicate state name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kilgore%2C_Nebraska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Kilgore thrived from the 1900s to the late 1920s, boasting hotels, a livery stable, and a bustling grain elevator.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kilgore%2C_Nebraska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Two passenger trains ran each way daily through Kilgore, linking the village to regional markets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
